// Auth0CustomDomain is a component that serves an Auth0 tenant's sign-in on a
// domain you own -- id.example.com instead of example.eu.auth0.com -- so Universal
// Login, the emails' links and the tokens' issuer all carry your name.
//
// Creating the domain is the first of three steps. Auth0 answers with the DNS
// record that proves you control the name (status.outputs.dns_record_name,
// dns_record_type, dns_record_value); a DNS record kind publishes it, and an
// Auth0CustomDomainVerification then waits until Auth0 confirms the record and
// issues the domain's certificate. The three compose in one install: the record
// reads the domain's outputs, and the verification depends on the record.
//
// Use cases:
// - Sign people in on your own domain, with Auth0 managing the certificate
// - Front the domain with your own proxy and certificate (Enterprise plan)
// - Give passkeys a relying-party identifier of your choosing
//
// Example manifest:
// ```yaml
// apiVersion: auth0.planton.dev/v1alpha1
// kind: Auth0CustomDomain
// metadata:
//
//	name: sign-in-domain
//	org: my-organization
